    21:11[pjb@thalassa org.xquartz:0 bocl-master 31Gi]$



0- the option must be named --on-error, not -on-error.
   short options start with a single dash: -f
   long options start with two dashes: --foo

1- --ON-ERROR DEBUG must call INVOKE-DEBUGGER upon error.

2- there must not be any such interaction between bocl and the surrounding shell
   (I guess this goes thru escape codes and the terminal? or some other way?
   here the test is in emacs M-x shell)

3- INVOKE-DEBUGGER must implement a "minimal" CL debugger with the following features:

     - display the error message (princ the condition parameter),

     - print the backtrace (obtained from the kernel, see below),

     - print the list of currently available restarts (restart menu),

     - debugging REPL:
        - read a command:
            - if it's a debugger command, execute it,
            - if it's some lisp expression, evaluate it and print the results,
        - and loop

     - an easy way to distinguish debugger commands from lisp expressions is to
       use keywords for debugger commands and restart numbers. eg:

       :backtrace :type call :verbose 1  :count 10
       :backtrace :type binding :verbose 2 :start 20 :count 10
       :backtrace :type all :verbose 0

       :restart 2
       :restart try-again
       :restart abort-connection

       :continue
       :abort
       :quit

       Non-debugger command keywords would evaluate as lisp expressions.

     - debugger commands:
         - select a restart and invoke-restart it.
           (note if the restart is interactive this may lead to some interactive I/O
           to fill the parameters for the restart),
           The restart can be selected by "name" or by number in the restart menu.
         - print the backtrace again (with options, such as range of frames, verbose or not).
         - continue, ie. return NIL from invoke-debugger.
         - abort, ie. non-local exit back to the toplevel REPL.
         - quit, ie. non-local exit from the toplevel REPL, aka QUIT.

     There is no provision to perform stepping or tracing execution
     from the debugger (the user can use CL:TRACE and CL:BREAK).

     And there is no provision yet to inspect or modify the stack
     frames in the backtrace.

     The debugger REPL evaluates the expressions in the dynamic
     context of the function that calls INVOKE-DEBUGGER (or ERROR,
     CERROR, or SIGNAL), but not the lexical context.

     The expressions given at the debugger REPL can modify the state
     of the dynamic variables and of the lisp objects that are bound
     in the lexical context of the function (but not the lexical
     variables).

   So INVOKE-DEBUGGER implements a minimal debugger that can be
   implemented entirely and conformingly in Common Lisp.

   The kernel provide a list of frame from the top-of-the-stack down
   to the bottom (the call ot bocl from the main).
   For now, each frame is an opaque object.

   (bocl:backtrace) --> list-of-frames (first is top-of-the-stack)
   (bocl:frame-kind frame) -> keyword denoting the kind of frame
   (bocl:frame-name frame) -> function name (lambda expression) or special operator or nil
   (bocl:print-frame frame stream :verbosity vlevel)
   verbosity-level = 0 just print the frame kind and name
   verbosity-level = 1 print the parameter names (function) or the variable names (let), without values.
   verbosity-level = 2 print also the values bound to the parameters or the variables, subject to
   the printer variables as bound by INVOKE-DEBUGGER from the bocl:*debugger-print-…* variables.


   | debugger variable                  | default value | Printer variable bound by INVOKE-DEBUGGER |
   |------------------------------------+---------------+-------------------------------------------|
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-ARRAY*        | T             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-ARRAY*                 |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-BASE*         | 10            | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-BASE*                  |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-CASE*         | :UPCASE       | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-CASE*                  |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-CIRCLE*       | T             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-CIRCLE*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-ESCAPE*       | T             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-ESCAPE*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-GENSYM*       | T             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-GENSYM*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-LENGTH*       | 8             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-LENGTH*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-LEVEL*        | 3             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-LEVEL*                 |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-LINES*        | 2             | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-LINES*                 |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-PRETTY*       | NIL           | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-PRETTY*                |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-RADIX*        | NIL           | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-RADIX*                 |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-READABLY*     | NIL           | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-READABLY*              |
   | BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-RIGHT-MARGIN* | 72            | COMMON-LISP:*PRINT-RIGHT-MARGIN*          |
   |------------------------------------+---------------+-------------------------------------------|



