PROGRESS (slice 46, v1.1.3) — KEPT OPEN (large feature)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
DONE:
- Point 0: the option is now named --on-error (two dashes); -on-error kept as a
  legacy alias (sources/main.c).

ALREADY PRESENT:
- Restart system: FIND-RESTART / INVOKE-RESTART / RESTART-CASE / COMPUTE-RESTARTS
  (sources/conditions.c, sources/eval.c, prelude). INVOKE-DEBUGGER and BREAK are
  currently STUBS (prelude: invoke-debugger just (error condition)).

REMAINING (the bulk — a real minimal debugger):
- Point 3: a kernel backtrace API — (bocl:backtrace) -> frames, (bocl:frame-kind),
  (bocl:frame-name), (bocl:print-frame frame stream :verbosity v). Requires C
  support to expose the call/binding stack as frame objects.
- A real INVOKE-DEBUGGER: print condition + backtrace + restart menu, then a
  debugger REPL with keyword commands (:backtrace, :restart N/name, :continue,
  :abort, :quit) and lisp-expression evaluation in the error's dynamic context.
- The 12 BOCL:*DEBUGGER-PRINT-* variables (table in this issue) bound around the
  CL:*PRINT-* variables by INVOKE-DEBUGGER.
- Point 1: wire --on-error debug to call INVOKE-DEBUGGER on an unhandled error.
- Point 2: investigate the emacs `M-x shell` input-pushback (bocl appears to exit
  and the typed line reaches bash) — likely terminal/EOF handling, reproduce
  under M-x shell.
